**Proof.**

Let $C_1$ denote the touchpoint of the incircle $w$ on side $AB$ (the third touchpoint, not named in the problem but implicit). We work in the projective plane and use the **pole–polar theory** with respect to $w$.

**Setup and key involutions.** Since $A$ is the intersection of the two tangent lines to $w$ at $B_1$ and $C_1$ (namely sides $CA$ and $AB$), the *perspectivity from* $A$ defines an involution $\varphi$ on $w$: for each $X \in w$, $\varphi(X)$ is the second intersection of line $AX$ with $w$. The tangent at $B_1$ passes through $A$, so $\varphi(B_1)=B_1$; likewise $\varphi(C_1)=C_1$. Thus $\varphi$ is the unique involution on $w$ with fixed points $B_1, C_1$, and by definition $\varphi(A_1) = A_2$.

Similarly, $B$ is the intersection of tangents at $A_1$ and $C_1$ (sides $BC$ and $AB$). The perspectivity from $B$ gives an involution $\psi$ on $w$ with fixed points $A_1, C_1$, and $\psi(B_1) = B_2$.

**The concurrency point.** Let $P = AB \cap A_1B_1$. Since $AB$ is the tangent to $w$ at $C_1$, the point $P$ lies on this tangent. The perspectivity from $P$ gives an involution $\iota$ on $w$: for each $X \in w$, $\iota(X)$ is the second intersection of $PX$ with $w$. Because $P$ lies on the tangent at $C_1$, the line $PC_1$ is tangent, so $\iota(C_1) = C_1$. Because $P$ lies on line $A_1B_1$, the line $PA_1$ meets $w$ at $A_1$ and $B_1$, so $\iota(A_1) = B_1$.

**Conjugation identity.** Consider the composition $\iota \circ \varphi \circ \iota$. This is an involution (conjugate of the involution $\varphi$ by the involution $\iota$). Its fixed points are the images under $\iota$ of the fixed points of $\varphi$:
$$\iota(B_1) = A_1, \qquad \iota(C_1) = C_1.$$
So $\iota \circ \varphi \circ \iota$ is an involution on $w$ with fixed points $A_1$ and $C_1$.

But $\psi$ is *also* an involution on $w$ with fixed points $A_1$ and $C_1$.

**Key fact:** An involution on a conic (equivalently, on $\mathbb{RP}^1$) is uniquely determined by its two fixed points. (In the tangent-half-angle parametrization $t = \tan(\theta/2)$, the unique involution with fixed points $t_1, t_2$ is $f(t) = \frac{(t_1+t_2)t - 2t_1 t_2}{2t - (t_1+t_2)}$, which is the unique trace-zero Möbius transformation fixing $t_1$ and $t_2$.)

Therefore $\iota \circ \varphi \circ \iota = \psi$, which gives $\iota \circ \varphi = \psi \circ \iota$.

**Conclusion.** Applying both sides to $A_1$:
$$\iota(\varphi(A_1)) = \psi(\iota(A_1)) \implies \iota(A_2) = \psi(B_1) = B_2.$$

The relation $\iota(A_2) = B_2$ means that $P$, $A_2$, and $B_2$ are collinear (by the definition of $\iota$ as the perspectivity from $P$). Since $P = AB \cap A_1B_1$, the three lines $AB$, $A_1B_1$, and $A_2B_2$ all pass through $P$.

Therefore $AB$, $A_1B_1$, $A_2B_2$ are concurrent at the point $C_3 = P$. $\blacksquare$

